Yes. And I think the clean move is not to encode 50 tropes as 50 unrelated tricks. Treat them as phenotypes generated by a much smaller operator algebra.

Your tight rope → trope observation is actually right on target: a trope is a well-worn path through possibility space. A tightrope is a constrained path through physical space. Both work because the allowable trajectory has been narrowed enough that the pattern becomes recognizable.

The invariant connecting nearly all of them is:

A system begins in one state, encounters a constraint/asymmetry, traverses a meaningful difference, and either preserves or changes some invariant.

Formally, something like:

S_{t+1}=T(S_t,\;C,\;I,\;O)

where:

* S_t = current story/system state
* C = constraints
* I = information introduced or withheld
* O = observer/agent interpretation
* T = transformation operator

But the interesting object isn’t just the new state. It’s the delta:

\Delta S = S_{t+1}-S_t

and then:

M = f(\Delta S,\;K)

where K is the invariant or continuity that lets the observer recognize what changed and what remained the same.

That is basically narrative.

No delta → nothing happened.

No continuity → nothing can be recognized as having changed.

So story lives in the tension between invariant and transformation.

That is also extremely Pantheon.

The trope algebra

Almost every trope I named can be reduced to combinations of a few primitives:

1. Goal
G:S\rightarrow S^*

Something wants to move the world toward a target.

MacGuffin, Quest, Chosen One.

2. Constraint
C(S,A)\rightarrow \{0,1\}

Some transitions are disallowed.

Catch-22, tightrope, forbidden knowledge, prophecy.

3. Hidden information
H = S_{\text{true}}-S_{\text{observed}}

Red herring, unreliable narrator, twist, Rashomon.

4. Delayed dependency

x_t \rightarrow y_{t+n}

Chekhov’s Gun, foreshadowing, callback, plant/payoff.

5. Recurrence

S_{t+n}\approx S_t

but:

A_{t+n}\neq A_t

Groundhog Day, Sisyphus, cyclic quests.

The world resets while the agent retains something.

6. Identity preservation

\Phi(S_t)=\Phi(S_{t+n})

even though:

S_t\neq S_{t+n}

Ship of Theseus, death/rebirth, transformation stories.

7. Perspective transformation

R_i(S)\neq R_j(S)

Rashomon, foil, double, unreliable narrator.

Same reality; different projection.

8. Cost

U_{\text{local}}>0,\qquad U_{\text{global}}<0

Faustian bargain, Pyrrhic victory, Monkey’s Paw, Icarus.

9. Asymmetric information/ability

K_A > K_B

but:

P(A\rightarrow B)\approx0

Cassandra.

Correct cognition; failed transmission.

10. Inversion

T(T(S))\approx S

or semantic reversal:

Meaning(S_{t+1})=-Meaning(S_t)

Twists, false victories, reversal, tricksters.

That’s already most of storytelling.

